Decatur, Rainsville, Town Creek, Estill Springs, Athens, Arab,\nFayetteville, Florence, Scottsboro, Moulton, Red Bay, Sheffield,\nLynchburg, Russellville, Boaz, Tuscumbia, Cullman, Guntersville,\nMuscle Shoals, Sewanee, Winchester, and Fort Payne\n1104 AM CDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\n...FLOOD WATCH IN EFFECT FROM LATE FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY\nMORNING...\n\n* WHAT...Flash flooding caused by excessive rainfall is possible.\n\n* WHERE...Portions of Alabama, including the following areas,\n  Colbert, Cullman, DeKalb, Franklin AL, Jackson, Lauderdale,\n  Lawrence, Limestone, Madison, Marshall and Morgan and southern\n  middle Tennessee, including the following areas, Franklin TN,\n  Lincoln and Moore.\n\n* WHEN...From late Friday night through Sunday morning.\n\n* IMPACTS...Excessive runoff may result in flooding of rivers,\n  creeks, streams, and other low-lying and flood-prone locations.\n  Storm drains and ditches may become clogged with debris.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n  - Heavy rainfall associated with Hurricane Isaias will move through\n    the area starting overnight Friday into Saturday and continue\n    through Sunday morning. Widespread 2-4 inches is expected with\n    locally higher amounts between 5-7 inches.\n  - http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood\n\nPRECAUTIONARY/PREPAREDNESS ACTIONS...\n\nYou should monitor later forecasts and be prepared to take action\nshould Flash Flood Warnings be issued.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nGH\n\n\u0003\n\n### nws/HLSMOB.txt\n453 \nWTUS84 KMOB 082116\nHLSMOB\nALZ051>060-261>266-FLZ201>206-MSZ067-075-076-078-079-090530-\n\nHurricane Isaias Local Statement Advisory Number 9\nNational Weather Service Mobile AL  AL092026\n416 PM CDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nThis product covers portions of southwest Alabama...northwest Florida...south \ncentral Alabama...and inland southeast Mississippi.\n\n**HURRICANE HUNTERS FIND THAT ISAIAS NOW HAS 100 MPH WINDS** \n**PREPARATIONS TO PROTECT LIFE AND PROPERTY FROM STORM SURGE AND\nDAMAGING WINDS IN THE WARNING AREA SHOULD BE RUSHED TO COMPLETION\nTODAY**\n\nNEW INFORMATION\n---------------\n\n* CHANGES TO WATCHES AND WARNINGS:\n    - None\n\n* CURRENT WATCHES AND WARNINGS:\n    - A Storm Surge Warning and Hurricane Warning are in effect for \n      Baldwin Central, Baldwin Coastal, Escambia Coastal, Mobile \n      Central, Mobile Coastal, Okaloosa Coastal, and Santa Rosa \n      Coastal\n    - A Tropical Storm Warning and Hurricane Watch are in effect for \n      Conecuh and Covington\n    - A Hurricane Warning is in effect for Baldwin Inland, Escambia, \n      Escambia Inland, Mobile Inland, Okaloosa Inland, and Santa Rosa \n      Inland\n    - A Tropical Storm Warning is in effect for Butler, Clarke, \n      Crenshaw, George, Greene, Monroe, Washington, and Wilcox\n    - A Tropical Storm Watch is in effect for Choctaw, Perry, Stone, \n      and Wayne\n\n* STORM INFORMATION:\n    - About 440 miles south of Mobile AL or about 440 miles \n      south-southwest of Pensacola FL\n    - 24.4N 89.3W\n    - Storm Intensity 100 mph\n    - Movement Northeast or 50 degrees at 12 mph\n\nSITUATION OVERVIEW\n------------------\n\nHurricane Isaias is forecast to continue strengthening through\nFriday morning as it moves northeast into the central Gulf. The\nhurricane will then turn northward and accelerate toward the north\ncentral Gulf Coast. Some weakening of the peak winds will likely occur\njust before landfall as the storm encounters increasing wind shear.\nHowever, Isaias is expected to still be a dangerous hurricane at\nlandfall Friday night. It is too early to pinpoint the landfall\nlocation, but it is expected across either coastal Alabama or the\nwestern Florida Panhandle. It is important not to focus just on the\ncenter as the wind field will expand by landfall, resulting in the\npotential for significant wind impacts and a life threatening storm\nsurge along and to the east of where the hurricane makes landfall.\n\nPeak storm surge inundation of 5 to 7 feet above normally dry ground\nis possible near and to the east of where the center makes landfall.\nAreas that are west of the center will see a strong north wind and\nbelow normal water levels. Evacuation orders have been issued by some\ncoastal counties and you should check with your local emergency\nmanagement agency for the latest information. \n\nThose in the hurricane warning need to prepare for damaging hurricane\nforce winds of 74 to 110 mph, especially across coastal Alabama and\nthe western Florida Panhandle. The strongest winds will occur near and\njust to the east of the center. Potential impacts from these winds\ninclude numerous downed trees and power lines, significant power\noutages, and some roof damage. Tropical storm force winds are likely\nto spread well inland, at least in frequent gusts, as the center of\nIsaias quickly moves north. \n\nRainfall totals of 4 to 8 inches are expected across southwest\nALabama, south central Alabama, and the western Florida Panhandle.\nLocalized amounts of 10 to 15 inches are possible in bands across the\n\n### nws/HLSBMX.txt\n165 \nWTUS84 KBMX 082115\nHLSBMX\nALZ011>015-017>050-090515-\n\nHurricane Isaias Local Statement Advisory Number 9\nNational Weather Service Birmingham AL  AL092026\n415 PM CDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nThis product covers Central Alabama\n\n**TROPICAL STORM WARNINGS ISSUED FOR SOUTHERN PORTIONS OF CENTRAL\n ALABAMA** \n\nNEW INFORMATION\n---------------\n\n* CHANGES TO WATCHES AND WARNINGS:\n    - The Tropical Storm Watch has been upgraded to a Tropical Storm \n      Warning for Autauga, Barbour, Bullock, Dallas, Elmore, Lowndes, \n      Macon, Marengo, Montgomery, and Pike\n\n* CURRENT WATCHES AND WARNINGS:\n    - A Tropical Storm Warning is in effect for Autauga, Barbour, \n      Bullock, Dallas, Elmore, Lowndes, Macon, Marengo, Montgomery, \n      and Pike\n    - A Tropical Storm Watch is in effect for Bibb, Chambers, \n      Chilton, Clay, Coosa, Greene, Hale, Jefferson, Lee, Perry, \n      Pickens, Randolph, Russell, Shelby, St. Clair, Sumter, \n      Talladega, Tallapoosa, and Tuscaloosa\n\n* STORM INFORMATION:\n    - About 650 miles south-southwest of Birmingham AL or about 580 \n      miles south-southwest of Montgomery AL\n    - 24.4N 89.3W\n    - Storm Intensity 100 mph\n    - Movement Northeast or 50 degrees at 12 mph\n\nSITUATION OVERVIEW\n------------------\n\nHurricane Isaias will continue to move towards the Northern Gulf \nCoast, making landfall Friday evening. Winds will gust as high as 40 \nto 50 mph late Friday night through Saturday, with the potential for \nisolated higher gusts to 60 mph, resulting in the potential for \nnumerous downed trees and power lines. All of Central Alabama is \nunder Tropical Storm or High Wind watches and warnings. Rainfall \namounts of 1.5 to 4 inches with locally higher amounts up to 5 inches \nwill result in the potential for flash flooding, especially in urban \nand other poor drainage areas. A threat for isolated tornadoes may \ndevelop across portions of Central Alabama along and east of the track \non Saturday, mainly east of Interstate 65. Confidence is low to medium \nat this time.\n\nPOTENTIAL IMPACTS\n-----------------\n\n* WIND:\nProtect against dangerous wind having possible significant impacts\nacross southern portions of Central Alabama and higher elevations. \nPotential impacts in this area include:\n    - Some damage to roofing and siding materials, along with damage \n      to porches, awnings, carports, and sheds. A few buildings \n      experiencing window, door, and garage door failures. Mobile and \n      manufactured homes damaged, especially if unanchored. Unsecured \n      lightweight objects become dangerous projectiles.\n    - Several large trees snapped or uprooted. Several fences and \n      roadway signs blown over.\n    - Some roads impassable from large debris, and more within urban \n      or heavily wooded places.\n    - Scattered power and communications outages, but more prevalent \n      in areas with above ground lines.\n\nAlso, protect against hazardous wind having possible limited impacts \nacross the rest of Central Alabama.\n\n* FLOODING RAIN:\nPrepare for dangerous rainfall flooding having possible significant \nimpacts across Central Alabama. Potential impacts include:\n    - Moderate rainfall flooding may prompt several evacuations and \n\n### nws/HLSTAE.txt\n237 \nWTUS82 KTAE 082135\nHLSTAE\nALZ065>069-FLZ007>018-027-108-112-114-115-118-127-128-134-326-426-GAZ120>127-142>146-155>158-090545-\n\nHurricane Isaias Local Statement Advisory Number 9\nNational Weather Service Tallahassee FL  AL092026\n535 PM EDT Thu Oct 8 2026 /435 PM CDT Thu Oct 8 2026/\n\nThis product covers eastern Florida panhandle, Florida Big Bend, \nsoutheastern Alabama and southwestern Georgia.\n\n**HURRICANE HUNTERS FIND THAT ISAIAS NOW HAS 100 MPH WINDS** \n\nNEW INFORMATION\n---------------\n\n* CHANGES TO WATCHES AND WARNINGS:\n    - The Tropical Storm Warning and Hurricane Watch have been \n      upgraded to a Hurricane Warning for Central Walton and North \n      Walton\n\n* CURRENT WATCHES AND WARNINGS:\n    - A Hurricane Warning is in effect for Central Walton and North \n      Walton\n    - A Storm Surge Warning and Hurricane Warning are in effect for \n      Coastal Bay and South Walton\n    - A Storm Surge Warning and Tropical Storm Warning are in effect \n      for Coastal Franklin, Coastal Gulf, Coastal Jefferson, and \n      Coastal Wakulla\n    - A Storm Surge Warning is in effect for Coastal Dixie and \n      Coastal Taylor\n    - A Tropical Storm Warning is in effect for Calhoun, Clay, \n      Coffee, Dale, Decatur, Early, Gadsden, Geneva, Henry, Holmes, \n      Houston, Inland Bay, Inland Franklin, Inland Gulf, Inland \n      Wakulla, Jackson, Leon, Miller, Northern Liberty, Seminole, \n      Southern Liberty, and Washington\n    - A Tropical Storm Watch is in effect for Baker, Calhoun, \n      Colquitt, Dougherty, Grady, Inland Jefferson, Lee, Mitchell, \n      Quitman, Randolph, Terrell, Thomas, and Worth\n\n* STORM INFORMATION:\n    - About 450 miles south-southwest of Panama City or about 450 \n      miles southwest of Apalachicola\n    - 24.4N 89.3W\n    - Storm Intensity 100 mph\n    - Movement Northeast or 50 degrees at 12 mph\n\nSITUATION OVERVIEW\n------------------\n\n...INLAND WALTON COUNTY UPGRADED TO HURRICANE WARNING...\n...CONSIDERABLE FLOODING RAIN THREAT EXPECTED FOR FLORIDA PANHANDLE...\n\nAt 5 PM ET, Hurricane Isaias was located in the southern Gulf with\nmaximum sustained winds of 100 mph and moving NE at 12 mph. There are\noverall no significant changes in the forecast reasoning from the\nprevious advisory. Isaias is forecast to turn northward in the next\nday or so and accelerate in forward speed towards the central Gulf\ncoast. Additional strengthening is anticipated through tonight, and\nmay be near major hurricane strength prior to landfall. The wind field\nis expected to expand considerably and may be lopsided towards the\neast, which means impacts are likely well beyond the forecast cone,\nparticularly east of the hurricane's center. \n\nConditions could begin to deteriorate across the Florida Panhandle as\nearly as Friday morning. Therefore, residents are urged to finalize\ntheir preparations today and early tomorrow.\n\nGiven the expected large size of Isaias as it nears northern Gulf\ncoast, the wind threat may extend well into the eastern portions of\nthe Tri-state area. An expected fast forward speed may also lead to\nthe wind threat extending further inland, especially near the core of\nthe storm. A Storm Surge Warning remains in effect from the all of our\nPanhandle and Big Bend coast. Life-threatening inundation values of\n5 to 7 feet above normally dry ground are forecast across the coastal\nPanhandle to Indian Pass, 4 to 6 feet from Indian Pass to the\nSteinhatchee River, and 3 to 5 feet from the Steinhatchee River to\nSuwannee River, coincident with the high tide. The greatest surge risk\nwill be Friday into Saturday.\n\n### nhc/TCPAT4_20261008T2351Z_KNHC_adv9A.txt\n136 \nWTNT34 KNHC 082351\nTCPAT4\n \nBULLETIN\nHurricane Isaias Intermediate Advisory Number 9A\nNWS National Hurricane Center Miami FL       AL092026\n700 PM CDT Thu Oct 08 2026\n \n...PREPARATIONS TO PROTECT LIFE AND PROPERTY FROM STORM SURGE AND \nDAMAGING WINDS SHOULD BE RUSHED TO COMPLETION IN THE WARNING AREA...\n \n \nSUMMARY OF 700 PM CDT...0000 UTC...INFORMATION\n----------------------------------------------\nLOCATION...24.9N 88.8W\nABOUT 255 MI...410 KM NNE OF PROGRESO MEXICO\nABOUT 290 MI...470 KM S OF THE MOUTH OF THE MISSISSIPPI RIVER\nMAXIMUM SUSTAINED WINDS...100 MPH...155 KM/H\nPRESENT MOVEMENT...NE OR 45 DEGREES AT 13 MPH...20 KM/H\nMINIMUM CENTRAL PRESSURE...974 MB...28.77 INCHES\n \n \nWATCHES AND WARNINGS\n--------------------\nCHANGES WITH THIS ADVISORY:\n \nNone.\n \nSUMMARY OF WATCHES AND WARNINGS IN EFFECT:\n \nA Storm Surge Warning is in effect for...\n* Mouth of the Mississippi River to the Suwannee River\n \nA Hurricane Warning is in effect for...\n* Ocean Springs, MS to the Bay/Gulf County Line, FL\n \nA Tropical Storm Warning is in effect for...\n* Jefferson/Plaquemines Parish Line, LA to west of Ocean Springs, MS\n* East of Bay/Gulf County Line to Aucilla River, FL\n \nA Storm Surge Watch is in effect for...\n* Suwannee River to Yankeetown, FL\n \nA Storm Surge Warning means there is a danger of life-threatening\ninundation, from rising water moving inland from the coastline,\nduring the next 36 hours in the indicated locations. For a depiction\nof areas at risk, please see the National Weather Service Storm\nSurge Watch/Warning Graphic, available at hurricanes.gov. This is a\nlife-threatening situation. Persons located within these areas\nshould take all necessary actions to protect life and property from\nrising water and the potential for other dangerous conditions.\nPromptly follow evacuation and other instructions from local\nofficials.\n \nA Hurrica